Title = {Transitional changes in the CRP structure lead to the exposure of
   proinflammatory binding sites},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2017},
Volume = {8},
Month = {JAN 23},
Abstract = {C-reactive protein (CRP) concentrations rise in response to tissue
   injury or infection. Circulating pentameric CRP (pCRP) localizes to
   damaged tissue where it leads to complement activation and further
   tissue damage. In-depth knowledge of the pCRP activation mechanism is
   essential to develop therapeutic strategies to minimize tissue injury.
   Here we demonstrate that pCRP by binding to cell-derived microvesicles
   undergoes a structural change without disrupting the pentameric symmetry
   (pCRP{*}). pCRP{*} constitutes the major CRP species in human-inflamed
   tissue and allows binding of complement factor 1q (C1q) and activation
   of the classical complement pathway. pCRP{*}-microvesicle complexes lead
   to enhanced recruitment of leukocytes to inflamed tissue. A
   small-molecule inhibitor of pCRP (1,6-bis(phosphocholine)-hexane), which
   blocks the pCRP-microvesicle interactions, abrogates these
   proinflammatory effects. Reducing inflammation-mediated tissue injury by
   therapeutic inhibition might improve the outcome of myocardial
   infarction, stroke and other inflammatory conditions.},
